# Static analysis configuration.
#
# The point of this file is to convert the advice in CODING_GUIDELINES.md into
# something a machine enforces on every build, because advice that relies on
# everyone remembering decays and advice a tool checks does not.
#
# Every disabled check has a reason and a count of what turning it back on
# would cost. If you disable one more, write both -- a bare list of
# suppressions is how a lint config quietly becomes meaningless.
#
# They are in the block below rather than beside the entries themselves,
# because YAML will not allow the latter: `Checks:` is a folded block scalar,
# so a `#` inside it is not a comment, it is text. Tried on 2026-09-10 --
# clang-tidy --verify-config then reports
#
#     unknown check '# a comment inside the folded block -readability-...'
#
# because the comment and the next entry fold into one name, **and the
# suppression it was attached to silently stops applying**. A comment that
# turns a check back on without saying so is worse than no comment.
#
# The list is meant to shrink. It was emptied on 2026-09-08 to see what was
# actually in it; ten of the fourteen entries turned out to be either dead,
# cheap to fix, or better expressed as a NOLINT at the handful of sites that
# needed one, and are gone.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The four that remain, and what each costs.
#
# **This is the only place the costs are quoted.** They were in two blocks in
# this file until 2026-09-13, one dated and one not, and the two disagreed --
# 276/116 against 277/151, 414/194 against 408/219, 365/174 against 357/172.
# That is STATUS.md's lesson arriving inside the lint config: a number written
# twice is one fact with two chances to rot. The second block now carries the
# reasoning only, and points here for the numbers.
#
# Measured 2026-09-22 over the **twenty-three** linted translation units, with
# `--checks=-*,<check>` over each. **This block is a dated snapshot, and the
# translation-unit count has gone stale three times** -- it said five, then
# nine, then twenty-two, each time because the linted set grew with a new suite
# rather than because the code got worse. Re-measure before quoting it.
#
# "findings" is what a lint run prints, which counts a header once per
# translation unit that includes it; "lines" is distinct source lines, which is
# what a NOLINT at every site would have to touch. **Read the lines, not the
# findings** -- between 2026-09-13 and 2026-09-22 the findings roughly
# quadrupled while the lines not quite doubled, and almost all of that gap is
# the same header counted in more translation units.
#
# To re-measure, and check the instrument first: under `WarningsAsErrors: '*'`
# a finding prints as `error: ... [<check>,-warnings-as-errors]`, **not** as
# `warning: ... [<check>]`. A grep for the second form returns zero for every
# check and looks exactly like a clean tree. That happened while taking this
# measurement -- VERIFICATION.md rule 23, distrust a null result, arriving
# inside the file it is about.
#
#   -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-magic-numbers    3,270 findings, 534 lines
#   -readability-magic-numbers                the same 3,270 -- these are two
#                                             names for one check, so the cost
#                                             is 3,270 and not 6,540
#
#       326 of the 534 lines are in tests/, where a table of expected values
#       *is* the test: naming each entry would hide the thing being asserted.
#       The other 208 are in src/ (129 core, 50 orbit, 21 view, 5 astro,
#       3 app), and most are a series coefficient or a conversion factor whose
#       provenance is already in a comment beside it. This is the entry most
#       worth removing, and the cheapest, because the two populations want
#       opposite treatment and clang-tidy reads a .clang-tidy per directory.
#
#   -readability-identifier-length             3,064 findings, 584 lines
#
#       The check wants three characters and this domain's notation does not
#       have them. 74 distinct names; the common ones are el (54), a (44),
#       v (43), m (39), tt (38), t (34), q (24), c (24), sv (24), ok (22),
#       e (20), mu (19), s (18), x (16), r (15), b (12). Renaming `a` to
#       `semiMajorAxis` inside a Kepler solve makes the formula harder to check
#       against the book it came from, not easier, and CODING_GUIDELINES
#       section 15 asks for names that say what a thing is in its own domain.
#       The narrower instrument here is this check's own IgnoredParameterNames /
#       IgnoredVariableNames, not a NOLINT: see .claude/rules/lint-config.md.
#       By directory: 312 lines in tests, 119 core, 84 orbit, 33 view,
#       23 render, 13 astro.
#
#   -modernize-use-trailing-return-type        3,005 findings, 509 lines
#
#       A style inversion, not a defect. The check wants `auto f() -> T`; this
#       codebase writes `T f()`, consistently, in all 509 places. Zero of the
#       3,005 is a bug. It is the same class of thing as the brace deviation in
#       CODING_GUIDELINES section 15 -- choose one, apply it everywhere, write
#       it down -- so turning it on means rewriting 509 declarations and
#       changing the house style. That is a decision about taste.
#       By directory: 169 core, 160 tests, 71 render, 48 orbit, 29 view,
#       23 astro, 9 app.
---
Checks: >
  clang-analyzer-*,
  bugprone-*,
  cert-*,
  concurrency-*,
  cppcoreguidelines-*,
  modernize-*,
  performance-*,
  portability-*,
  readability-*,
  misc-*,

  -cppcoreguidelines-avoid-magic-numbers,
  -readability-magic-numbers,
  -readability-identifier-length,
  -modernize-use-trailing-return-type,

# Only our code. The dependency tree under _deps is not ours to lint, and
# linting it buries every finding that matters.
#
# Both separators, deliberately. clang-tidy on Windows reports a header as
# `src\core/Math.hpp` -- the compile database's forward slashes up to the
# include directory, then the backslash the driver joined the include path
# with. A regex that only accepts `/` matched nothing, and every header in
# this tree went unlinted for the first sixteen commits while the .cpp files
# reported clean. If this ever regresses, `--header-filter='.*'` on one
# translation unit will show findings this filter does not.
HeaderFilterRegex: '.*[/\\](src|tests)[/\\].*\.(hpp|h)$'
ExcludeHeaderFilterRegex: '.*_deps.*'

# The tree is clean under this configuration, so the findings are errors: the
# count can now only go down. If that ever becomes impractical, narrow this to
# the highest-signal families rather than emptying it -- a lint config nobody
# has to satisfy is a lint config nobody reads.
WarningsAsErrors: '*'

CheckOptions:
  # Matches the naming rules in section 15 of CODING_GUIDELINES.md.
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.NamespaceCase
    value: lower_case
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ClassCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.StructCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.EnumCase
    value: CamelCase
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.FunctionCase
    value: camelBack
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.VariableCase
    value: camelBack
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.PrivateMemberSuffix
    value: _
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.GlobalConstantPrefix
    value: k
  - key: readability-identifier-naming.ConstexprVariablePrefix
    value: k

  # Section 17: functions should fit on a screen. VulkanContext::init() used to
  # be 149 lines doing eight jobs; it is now 29 and delegates to five named
  # steps, which is what this threshold is here to keep true.
  - key: readability-function-size.LineThreshold
    value: '80'
  - key: readability-function-size.StatementThreshold
    value: '60'
  - key: readability-function-size.NestingThreshold
    value: '5'

  # The single-line guard clause (`if (!path) return;`) is the documented house
  # style; a body that runs onto its own line is not, and gets braces. This
  # value expresses that distinction, which is why the check is configured here
  # rather than disabled: it was disabled outright until 2026-09-08, and turning
  # it back on with this threshold left exactly three real findings, all of them
  # two-line bodies without braces, all now fixed.
  - key: readability-braces-around-statements.ShortStatementLines
    value: '1'

  # Non-negotiable 1 has exactly one mechanical half, and by default it was
  # blind in a way nobody had measured. The check silences any pair of
  # same-typed parameters that appear together in one expression, which is most
  # of them: `at(row, column)` returning `e.at((column * 4) + row)` was silent,
  # and so were a date's three integers, a range's two bounds and a layout
  # transition's two ends. Switched off 2026-09-20, with the whole tree fixed
  # rather than re-suppressed.
  #
  # Measured that day over the twenty linted translation units, before any
  # change: 22 findings on 22 distinct source lines in 11 files, where the
  # default reported 0. Of those, 13 were real -- a date, four adjacent
  # Degrees, a layout transition, three inverted ranges, a denominator, a
  # before/after pair, A*B^T, and two signed differences -- and each was fixed
  # by giving the pair a name, so a call site now says which is which. The
  # other 9 are provably symmetric (`a + b`, `a * b`, `|a - b|`, a dot product)
  # and carry a NOLINT with that reason at the site, which is the wording
  # core/Scalar.hpp already uses on nearlyEqual. crossExact is the one
  # exception: it is antisymmetric and suppressed anyway, because (a, b) is the
  # notation every reader of a cross product knows.
  #
  - key: bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters.SuppressParametersUsedTogether
    value: 'false'
  # The *other* blind spot, closed 2026-09-21. At the default of 1 the check is
  # silent on any pair whose names share a one-character prefix or suffix, so
  # `V(f64 x_, f64 y_, f64 z_)` is silent while `V(f64 alpha, f64 beta, f64
  # gamma)` fires -- identical signatures, only the names differ. At 0 the whole
  # tree reports **four** sites, each now carrying a NOLINT with its reason at
  # the site: Vec3's two constructors and Quat's in core/Math.hpp, and
  # WithinRelVecBase in tests/OrbitTestSupport.hpp. All four are canonical
  # orderings, so nothing was a defect -- what the option buys is that the
  # *fifth* one breaks the build instead of going unnoticed.
  #
  # It was left at the default until now on a measurement of 2026-09-13 that
  # found three sites. Re-measured 2026-09-21: four. Two had appeared with
  # ADR 0019 on 2026-09-17 -- Vec3's Component constructor and
  # WithinRelVecBase -- and Orbit.cpp's stumpff, the one real finding of the
  # three, had been fixed. The old note ended "if a fourth site ever appears,
  # the option is how to find it"; a fourth appeared four days later and
  # nothing ran it, which is the argument for turning the option on rather than
  # keeping a number that has to be re-measured by hand.
  - key: bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters.NamePrefixSuffixSilenceDissimilarityThreshold
    value: '0'

# --- notes on the disabled checks -------------------------------------------
#
# This list was fourteen checks long until 2026-09-08, when it was emptied and
# every finding that came out of it was fixed rather than re-suppressed. Ten
# entries went: the eight below, because they were dead or cheap to satisfy,
# and two more (the pro-type-* pair at the end of this file) that moved to a
# NOLINT at each of the five sites that actually needed one.
#
# What the eight cost is recorded here so nobody re-adds them:
#
#   bugprone-narrowing-conversions            0 findings. -Wconversion already
#   cppcoreguidelines-narrowing-conversions   0 findings.   covers this.
#   cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-array-to-pointer-decay   0 findings.
#   modernize-use-nodiscard                   0 findings; the attribute is
#                                             already on everything it wants.
#   performance-enum-size                     5 enums given an explicit
#                                             std::uint8_t base, as interface
#                                             rather than optimisation.
#   cppcoreguidelines-pro-bounds-pointer-arithmetic
#                                             5 findings, all fixed: argv is a
#                                             std::span, the SDL extension list
#                                             is iterated rather than indexed,
#                                             and from_chars takes
#                                             std::to_address(text.end()).
#   readability-braces-around-statements      114 -> 3 with ShortStatementLines
#                                             above; the 3 were real.
#   misc-include-cleaner                      256 findings, all fixed. Worth the
#                                             churn: this is the check that
#                                             would have caught the missing
#                                             <tuple> that only gcc found. See
#                                             PROJECT_STATE.md section 3.
#
# The four below still fire. Each says why it is off; **the counts are in the
# block at the top of this file and nowhere else**, because until 2026-09-13
# they were in both places and the two had drifted apart.
#
# *-magic-numbers
#   In orbital mechanics, 2.0, 0.5 and 180.0 are mathematics, not magic. The
#   constants that DO deserve names (tolerances, iteration caps) are covered by
#   section 16, which asks for a comment saying where the number came from --
#   a requirement no linter can check. Most of the findings are literals in the
#   static_asserts and the test case tables, where naming each one moves the
#   value away from the assertion that gives it meaning.
#
# readability-identifier-length
#   dt, mu, e, r0, nu are the standard symbols of the domain. Renaming them to
#   satisfy a linter would make the code harder to check against a textbook.
#
#   The alternative to this line is NOT a NOLINT per site. The check takes a
#   name allow-list (IgnoredVariableNames / IgnoredParameterNames), and that is
#   the *narrower* instrument of the two: a NOLINT exempts a whole line, so a
#   badly named variable declared next to `mu` escapes with it, while the
#   allow-list exempts only those exact spellings and still reports a new `q2`
#   anywhere. The vocabulary it would have to spell out is 46 symbols:
#     a b c c0 c1 c2 c3 ci d dt e e2 el f g h hi k lo m mu n na nb nu ok p q r
#     r0 rc rp s sv t to u v v0 vp w w_ x x_ y_ z_
#   Some of those are domain notation and some (ok, to, na, nb) are just short.
#   Writing the list means deciding which is which, one name at a time.
#
# modernize-use-trailing-return-type
#   Pure style, and not the style used here. Satisfying it means rewriting
#   every function signature in the project as `auto f() -> T`.
#
# cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-reinterpret-cast and pro-type-vararg
#   NOT disabled here. Both were on this list until 2026-09-08; both are now
#   live, with a NOLINTNEXTLINE and a reason at each of the five sites that
#   need one -- three SDL_Log calls, the typed Vulkan entry point, and the
#   SPIR-V read. A suppression in this file would also cover the next
#   reinterpret_cast anybody writes; five at the sites cover exactly five lines.
#
# bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters
#   The mechanical enforcement of I.24 and section 2. It fired on
#   propagate(sv, mu, dt) and on three test helpers, which is exactly what it is
#   for: those were real defects, and they were fixed by introducing Radians,
#   Seconds, GravParam and Tolerance rather than by suppressing the check.
#
#   **It had two blind spots. The larger one is closed as of 2026-09-20**, by
#   the SuppressParametersUsedTogether option above -- the check was silent on
#   any pair used together in one expression, which hid 22 lines including a
#   date, a layout transition and three inverted ranges. The counts and what
#   happened to each are in the comment beside that option.
#
#   **The second is closed as of 2026-09-21**, and the way it was found is
#   worth more than the finding. Parameter names that share a one-character
#   prefix or suffix silence the check. Reproduced on a standalone probe:
#   `f(f64 psi, f64& c2, f64& c3)` is silent while
#   `f(f64 psi, f64& alpha, f64& beta)` fires, and `V(f64 x_, f64 y_, f64 z_)`
#   is silent while `V(f64 alpha, f64 beta, f64 gamma)` fires -- identical
#   signatures, only the names differ. The lever is this check's
#   NamePrefixSuffixSilenceDissimilarityThreshold, set to 0 above.
#
#   It was left at its default until then, on a measurement of 2026-09-13 that
#   found **3 lines**: Math.hpp's Vec3 and Quat constructors, and Orbit.cpp's
#   stumpff. The note ended: "if a fourth site ever appears, the option is how
#   to find it." **A fourth appeared four days later and nothing ran it.**
#   Re-measured 2026-09-21: four sites. stumpff, the one real finding of the
#   three, had been fixed by hand and returns a pair now; two new ones had
#   arrived with ADR 0019 on 2026-09-17, Vec3's Component constructor and
#   tests/OrbitTestSupport.hpp's WithinRelVecBase.
#
#   All four are canonical orderings that a call site shows plainly, so none is
#   a defect and each carries a NOLINT with that reason at the site rather than
#   an IgnoredParameterNames entry, which would exempt every future parameter
#   of that name instead of these four lines. What the option buys is that the
#   fifth site fails the build instead of waiting for somebody to remember a
#   number -- which is the whole argument of ADR 0005, applied to this file.
